# Harness hook files, written by `seraph install`.
#
# They embed the absolute path of the binary that wrote them — `/home/<you>/go/bin/seraph`
# — so a copy committed here would point at another machine's home directory and quietly do
# nothing. That is the failure TASK-110 spent a day on, not a hypothetical one. `seraph
# install` regenerates each of these per machine, which is why they are ignored rather than
# committed. This also matches the project's own rule, from prd.md §10: `.mcp.json` is
# committed with a bare `seraph` command precisely because an absolute path breaks on every
# other machine.
.claude/settings.json
.cursor/hooks.json
# `.kiro/hooks/` and `.omp/hooks/` are leftovers from before TASK-114: Kiro cannot enforce
# and is reported advisory, and omp's gate moved to ~/.omp/agent/hooks/, the only directory
# omp discovers. They are ignored rather than deleted because removing a file from someone's
# machine is not this repository's call.
.kiro/hooks/
.omp/hooks/

# The compiled binary. `go build` in the repository root writes ./seraph, which is the same
# name as the module and would sit beside go.mod looking like source.
/seraph

# Coverage and profiling output, if anyone reaches for them.
/coverage.out
*.test

# Local tooling configuration, not part of the product.
.impeccable/